sections.s2_technical:
When an object is serialized, the class descriptor in the stream includes the serialVersionUID. When those bytes are deserialized, the engine loads the current class, reads its serialVersionUID, and compares it to the one in the stream. If they are equal, deserialization proceeds; if they differ, it throws `java.io.InvalidClassException` with a message that shows both ids.

If you do not declare the field, the compiler computes a default value by hashing the class name, its implemented interfaces, and its fields and methods. The problem is that this computed value is extremely sensitive: adding a method, changing a field's access modifier, or many other edits change it. So two builds that are perfectly compatible in practice can produce different auto-generated ids, and old serialized data suddenly fails to load.

Declaring it explicitly fixes this:

```java
public class Account implements java.io.Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L; // you control compatibility
    private String accountId;
    private java.math.BigDecimal balance;
}
```

Now you decide. As long as you keep the id at 1L and make only compatible changes, old bytes still load. Compatible changes include adding a new field, which will simply be set to its default value when reading older data that lacks it, and removing a field, which means older data for that field is ignored. Incompatible changes, such as changing a field's type or the class hierarchy in a breaking way, should be accompanied by bumping the id so old data is rejected deliberately rather than misread.

A senior point is that serialVersionUID is a contract you manage. In a bank, serialized objects may sit in a cache or a queue across deployments, so when you release a new version of a class, you must consciously decide whether old in-flight data is still readable. Keeping the id stable across compatible changes avoids needless failures, while bumping it on breaking changes turns a silent corruption risk into a loud, safe InvalidClassException. Another senior point is to always declare it, because relying on the auto-generated value is the most common cause of mysterious InvalidClassException in production after a routine refactor.

